A||||10||wkayl||Lack of security in my opinion (personal experience)||||1||
Z||000000||wkayl||09-16-2001||09:37 AM||||In my line of work I drive a full size cargo van.  Saturday I had a service call at Denver International Airport.  At the first check gate the guard looked at my badge as usual, smiled and said have a good day.  There was an extra checkpoint just before I drove over the first taxiway.  The guard there again looked at my badge making sure I matched the picture.  She asked me if I had my lunch in the cooler on the seat next to me.  I said no, just Diet Cokes.  She let me pass.  At no time did anyone search my van that in the cargo area only has windows in the back and 2 on the pass side doors.  I drove my van alongside the concourse and parked within 20 ft of large airliners.  I carded into the concourse without any problem.  The worst part of my visit (in my opinion) was when I went to leave, my badge did not work to let me take the elevator down to the tarmac.  After the second try, I called on the white phone.  The person just said to wipe off my card, and try again (Access Services who issues cards was closed for the weekend).  It still didn’t work so I tried the stairway card reader.  An alarm went off.  Procedure is when this happens to “STAY PUT”, security will come.  I waited 15 min without anyone coming.  2 airline personnel came buy to use the elevator (which happened to go up to security). I asked them to send someone down.  After waiting another 5 minutes 2 guards showed up.  They took my card, put it in the reader, jerked it out, and the card worked.  They said there was no problem. They had me do it.  I inserted it, and pulled it back out normally.  It didn’t work.  They said I have to pull it out very quickly.  The attitude I got from them was that I was wasting their time.   <P>The overall experience was that in the light of what has happened this past week, more security should be taken to protect the public.<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/usa1017[1].gif">   <BR>Dnyee's 88 Formula, Casey's 86GT, Wayne's 86GT.  <BR>We consider my wife as being "Fiero Challenged"||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||09-16-2001||10:20 AM||||Don't you imagine that stuff like this happens all the time?<BR>People are too lazy to get up off their asses and do anything.<BR>That's what you get for $6.00 an hour.<BR>Scary.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||8
Z||000002||maryjane||09-16-2001||10:29 AM||||This incident needs to be reported to the gov't agency in your state, or on a federal level, that is responsible for airport security. I know it may get you in a little hot water, but think how you will feel if an attack is carrried out from that location.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jake_Dragon||09-16-2001||11:14 AM||||My wife is a security guard, she made $9.50 at her last post. I would hope that airports pay more than that.<BR>I have seen advertisements for licensed armed guard in the $12.00 range. <BR>You do get what you pay for. My wife had to go through school after school and continues to go to school every year. <BR>Now I am not saying that some guards don't care, some people see security work as an easy way to make money, sit in a shack and try to stay awake. <BR>My wife takes her job very serious, she is the eyes and ears of the places she works.<BR>Physical security is left to guard that <B>are</B> trained and paid for it. <BR>Don't group all security guards in with the slackers you see doing this. It is <B>management</B> that needs to wakeup not the guards.<BR>Just my 2 cents.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://web.tampabay.rr.com/jakedragon" TARGET=_blank>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/logo.gif">        </A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfpole_4.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||09-16-2001||01:31 PM||||Jake,<P>My apologies. Didn't mean to slam on your wife. I realize that I did a dis-service to all the security filks who really DO care.  I tip my hat to all those folks who take their jobs seriously. <BR>I meant to slam on the slackers that Wkayl wrote about. Those folks (and their management) seem to need a serious adjustment.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||09-16-2001||02:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jake_Dragon:<BR><B>It is [b]management</B> that needs to wakeup not the guards.<BR>Just my 2 cents.<P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, your 2 cents is a couple pennys short.  I agree with everthing you said except for this last statement.  Yes, management needs to wake up.  Definately.  But the guards are also responsible and need to wake up too.  Don't pass the buck to management cuz they don't pay enough or don't care enough.  It's everyone's problem, management, guards, and the people the guards are supposed to be protecting.  Any one of them who sees something that isn't as it should be has a moral responsibility (IMO) to report it to the proper authorities and do what they can to fix the problem.<P>Security Guard needs to become less of a grunt job and recognized for what it truly is.  Most of the time, they just stand there so it looks like there's security, but if something goes wrong who gets the blame?  People and the industries who use private security need to realize the responsibility they're giving these people.  You (people in general) expect them to be Police when needed, and cheap labor when everything is ok.  That double-standard is at least partly responsible for the current situation in the private security industry.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><I><FONT COLOR="blue">The path of the righteous man is beset on all sides by the inequities of the selfish and the tyrranys of evil men. Blessed is he who in the name of charity and good will shepards the weak through the valley of darkness for he is truly his brothers keeper, and the finder of lost children. And I shall strike down upon thee with great vengence and furious anger, those who attempt to poison and destroy my brothers. And you will know my name is the lord when I lay my vengence upon thee. </I></FONT></FONT><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/9-11-01a.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Jake_Dragon||09-16-2001||04:49 PM||||Management has to police the guards so the ones that don't care find new work. It should be a job your proud of, not one people snicker at. Certification and training need to be part of the job. I am not blaming anyone, we are all in this together, but I have to ask who hires the slackers and keeps them in place when they screw up? My wife has turned in several guards for not doing their duty and some of them have been let go. I don't take offence at anything being said here this is just my opinion and what I have seen from where my wife works.<p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||09-16-2001||06:35 PM||||I personally think that it should be the militaries job to gaurd the airports entrances security in the passenger terminals.It should be treated like sentry duty on any goverment base or embassy.period ||||reg||9
Z||000008||theogre||09-16-2001||06:42 PM||||two words.... Lowest Bidder.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||09-16-2001||07:01 PM||||It is not and should not be the military's or the FBI's job to guard anything in the Continental U.S. It would only be a short step from that to a national police force. The FBI is an 'investigative' unit. period.<BR>The Armed Forces need to devote their assets and time to training for times such as the present. They've been tried in several different roles, such as border patrol, and drug interdiction. With the exception of the Coast Guard and some Air Force recon units, they have always gotten into trouble. Just too aggressive. You can't teach them to kill everything that threatens them one week, and expect them to adhere to civil law the next.<BR>Just my opinion. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||starfighter007@msn.com||09-16-2001||07:09 PM||||and that would be a bad thing if someone did not follow the law when they are in a airport.||||reg||9
